<!-- p.25 — three highlight questions on the key sentence. The verb is a single
     word; the subject and the object are graded as WHOLE PHRASES — every word
     of the phrase and nothing outside it.

     Grading the phrase as a phrase is the lesson. The next two screens ask
     students to hold a noun phrase together as one unit; accepting the head
     word by itself here would teach the opposite on the screen before.

     Order is VERB → SUBJECT → OBJECT, not S-V-O. The verb is the one part
     you can find without having identified anything else; once you have it,
     "who did it" and "what got done to" are questions about the verb. Asking
     for the subject first asks students to find it out of nowhere. -->
<Vertical id="temperance_gram_svo">
<Markdown id="temperance_gram_svo_md">
## Verb, Subject, Object

Three quick highlights on the key sentence — the verb first, then the subject,
then the object.

The verb is a **single word**. The subject and the object are **whole phrases**:
highlight every word of the phrase, and nothing outside it.
</Markdown>

<CapaProblem id="temperance_gram_verb_problem" title="Highlight the verb" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<TextSelectionGrader id="temperance_gram_verb_grader">
<TextSelectionInput id="temperance_gram_verb">
Highlight the verb — a single word.
---
Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class [exacerbated] class, ethnic, and religious tensions.
---
all: Right — "exacerbated" is the verb: it's what the intrusions did.
: The verb is the action word — here "exacerbated," what the intrusions did.
</TextSelectionInput>
</TextSelectionGrader>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_gram_verb_problem -->

<CapaProblem id="temperance_gram_subject_problem" title="Highlight the subject" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<TextSelectionGrader id="temperance_gram_subject_grader">
<TextSelectionInput id="temperance_gram_subject">
Highlight the subject — the entire subject phrase, every word of it.
---
[Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class] exacerbated class, ethnic, and religious tensions.
---
all: Right — the subject is the whole phrase "Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class." Its head noun is "intrusions"; the rest of the phrase tells you which intrusions.
: The subject is who or what does the action — the whole phrase "Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class," built around the noun "intrusions."
</TextSelectionInput>
</TextSelectionGrader>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_gram_subject_problem -->

<!-- The trailing period is {optional}: a student sweeping to the end of the
     sentence catches it, and stopping a highlight one character short of a
     period is not a grammar mistake. -->
<CapaProblem id="temperance_gram_object_problem" title="Highlight the object" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<TextSelectionGrader id="temperance_gram_object_grader">
<TextSelectionInput id="temperance_gram_object">
Highlight the direct object — the entire object phrase, every word of it.
---
Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class exacerbated [class, ethnic, and religious tensions]{.}
---
all: Right — the object is the whole phrase "class, ethnic, and religious tensions." Its head noun is "tensions"; the words in front of it say which tensions.
: The object is what the intrusions exacerbated — the whole phrase "class, ethnic, and religious tensions," built around the noun "tensions."
</TextSelectionInput>
</TextSelectionGrader>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_gram_object_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_gram_svo -->

<!-- p.26 — inside the subject NP. ONE matching item, where three sequential
     highlights used to be. The parts on the right are the constituents of the
     phrase the student has just finished analyzing as a whole; naming them is
     recognition, and recognition of three related labels is one question, not
     three. Three separate one-attempt highlights would also mean three Submit
     buttons and three chances to be told nothing useful.

     Matching also carries partial credit, so a student who has the head noun
     and the postmodifier but not the determiner is scored as such.

     The phrase is shown WITHOUT the rest of the sentence: the rest of the
     sentence is nothing but a place to make mistakes here.

     "Determiner," not "premodifier" — it is the word the course actually uses
     for "Such," and it names a category rather than a position. -->
<Vertical id="temperance_gram_np">
<Markdown id="temperance_gram_np_md">
## Inside the Noun Phrase

The subject of that sentence is a noun phrase all by itself. Here it is on its
own — take it apart.
</Markdown>

<CapaProblem id="temperance_gram_np_problem" title="Inside the Noun Phrase" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<MatchingGrader id="temperance_gram_np_grader">
<Markdown id="temperance_gram_np_prompt">
Match each part of a noun phrase to the part that plays it in **Such intrusions
by the Protestant middle class**.
</Markdown>
<MatchingInput id="temperance_gram_np_match">
  <Markdown id="temperance_gram_np_role_head"> **Head noun** — the one word the whole phrase is built around                     </Markdown>
  <Markdown id="temperance_gram_np_part_head"> intrusions                                                                       </Markdown>

  <Markdown id="temperance_gram_np_role_det" > **Determiner** — the word before the head noun that helps identify or specify it </Markdown>
  <Markdown id="temperance_gram_np_part_det" > Such                                                                             </Markdown>

  <Markdown id="temperance_gram_np_role_post"> **Postmodifier** — the part after the head noun that tells you more about it     </Markdown>
  <Markdown id="temperance_gram_np_part_post"> by the Protestant middle class                                                   </Markdown>
</MatchingInput>
</MatchingGrader>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_gram_np_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_gram_np -->

<!-- p.27 — the structure, laid out (info screen) -->
<Vertical id="temperance_gram_structure">
<Markdown id="temperance_gram_structure_md">
## The Grammatical Structure

Here is the sentence laid out:

> **[ Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class ]** — subject (a noun phrase)
> **exacerbated** — verb
> **[ class, ethnic, and religious tensions ]** — direct object (a noun phrase)

Both the subject and the object are **noun phrases** — to understand the
sentence, you hold each whole phrase together.
</Markdown>
<Done id="temperance_gram_structure_done" label="I understand" />
</Vertical>

<!-- p.28 — the antecedent of "Such intrusions". The passage is split into
     sentences (separatorRegexp on the period; no abbreviations here, so the
     naive split is exact) and a sentence is the unit the student clicks:
     the answer is a whole sentence, and sweeping twenty-nine words to a
     pixel-exact period is a test of dragging, not of reading. -->
<SimpleTextSelection id="temperance_gram_antecedent" mode="graded" title="Find the Antecedent" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed" separatorRegexp="\.">
"Such intrusions" points back to an earlier sentence. Click the sentence that describes those intrusions — its antecedent.
---
In response to the perception that heavy drinking was associated with men who abused, abandoned, or neglected their family obligations, women formed a significant presence in societies dedicated to eradicating liquor. Temperance became a hallmark of middle-class respectability among both men and women and developed into a crusade with a visible class character. [Temperance, like many other reform efforts, was championed by the middle class and threatened to intrude on the private lives of lower-class workers, many of whom were Irish Catholics.] Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class exacerbated class, ethnic, and religious tensions. Still, while the temperance movement made less substantial inroads into lower-class workers' drinking culture, the movement was still a great success for reformers.
---
all: Yes — that earlier sentence describes the intrusion (the middle class threatening to intrude on lower-class, largely Irish Catholic workers) that "Such intrusions" refers back to.
: "Such intrusions" points to an intrusion already named — the sentence just before it, where temperance "threatened to intrude on the private lives of lower-class workers."
</SimpleTextSelection>


