<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — the five comprehension questions -->

<Vertical id="temperance_comp_1_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_comp_1_problem" title="Question 1" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_comp_1_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_comp_1">
<Markdown>
How did the Second Great Awakening address the rapid social and economic
changes of the post-independence era?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_1_prohibition_distilleries"  value="prohibition_distilleries" > By advocating for the prohibition of commercial distilleries.                      </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_1_traditional_protestantism" value="traditional_protestantism"> By promoting traditional Protestant Christianity while rejecting reform movements. </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_comp_1_revitalize_and_reform"     value="revitalize_and_reform"    > By revitalizing Christian spirits and inspiring personal and social reform.        </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_1_discourage_charity"        value="discourage_charity"       > By discouraging middle-class involvement in charitable organizations.              </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_comp_1_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The passage calls the Second Great Awakening "a spiritual response to such changes, revitalizing Christian spirits while also providing an institutional antidote... by inspiring both personal and social reform."*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_comp_1_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_comp_1_screen -->

<Vertical id="temperance_comp_2_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_comp_2_problem" title="Question 2" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_comp_2_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_comp_2">
<Markdown>
Why were middle-class evangelicals able to devote significant time and
resources to reform campaigns?
</Markdown>
         <Key id="temperance_comp_2_industrial_revolution_resources" value="industrial_revolution_resources"> The Industrial Revolution left them with time and resources to spare.       </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_2_churches_and_government"         value="churches_and_government"        > They were financially supported by their churches and by state governments. </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_2_tract_society_mandate"           value="tract_society_mandate"          > They were required to do so by the rules of the American Tract Society.     </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_2_laborers_funded"                 value="laborers_funded"                > Working-class laborers funded their reform efforts.                         </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_comp_2_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*"Because of the Industrial Revolution, middle-class evangelicals had time and resources to devote to reform campaigns."*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_comp_2_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_comp_2_screen -->

<Vertical id="temperance_comp_3_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_comp_3_problem" title="Question 3" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_comp_3_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_comp_3">
<Markdown>
How did temperance reformers shift their approach as alcoholism became an
increasingly visible issue in towns and cities?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_3_prohibition_to_moderation" value="prohibition_to_moderation"> They shifted from demanding total prohibition to encouraging moderate consumption.             </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_3_taxing_distilleries"       value="taxing_distilleries"      > They focused on taxing commercial distilleries rather than on getting people to stop drinking. </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_comp_3_moderation_to_abstinence"  value="moderation_to_abstinence" > They shifted from advocating moderation to demanding full abstinence from all alcohol.         </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_3_gave_up_bans"              value="gave_up_bans"             > They gave up on seeking legal bans on liquor sales and turned to persuasion.                   </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_comp_3_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*"Most reformers escalated their efforts from advocating moderation in liquor consumption to full abstinence from all alcohol."*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_comp_3_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_comp_3_screen -->

<Vertical id="temperance_comp_4_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_comp_4_problem" title="Question 4" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_comp_4_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_comp_4">
<Markdown>
What primary concern prompted many women to join societies dedicated to
stopping drinking alcohol?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_4_dairy_taxes"              value="dairy_taxes"             > They could no longer afford to pay the high taxes imposed on household dairy products like milk.     </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_comp_4_drinking_harmed_families" value="drinking_harmed_families"> They linked heavy drinking to men abusing, abandoning, or neglecting their families.                 </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_4_distillery_work"          value="distillery_work"         > The women wanted to work in the commercial distilleries, but state laws kept them out.               </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_4_opposed_beecher"          value="opposed_beecher"         > The women were opposed to the preaching of Lyman Beecher and the work of the American Tract Society. </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_comp_4_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Women formed a presence in these societies "in response to the perception that heavy drinking was associated with men who abused, abandoned, or neglected their family obligations."*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_comp_4_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_comp_4_screen -->

<Vertical id="temperance_comp_5_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_comp_5_problem" title="Question 5" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_comp_5_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_comp_5">
<Markdown>
What evidence from the text demonstrates the ultimate success of the
temperance movement by the 1840s?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_5_consumption_ended"          value="consumption_ended"         > Alcohol consumption ended in most towns and cities by the 1840s.       </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_5_legislatures_banned"        value="legislatures_banned"       > Most state legislatures banned the production of alcohol by the 1840s. </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_comp_5_workers_adopted_protestant" value="workers_adopted_protestant"> Lower-class workers adopted Protestant drinking culture.               </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_comp_5_half_of_1820s"              value="half_of_1820s"             > Americans in the 1840s drank half of what they had in the 1820s.       </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_comp_5_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*"In the 1840s, Americans drank half of what they had in the 1820s, and per capita consumption continued to decline over the next two decades."*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_comp_5_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_comp_5_screen -->


<!-- ============================================================
     Part 3 — The magazine article (PDF p.36+)
     The article is a copyrighted magazine piece, so it is NOT carried
     online: the instructor hands it out ON PAPER and students read and
     annotate the paper copy. This screen is the cue to pick it up; the
     article questions that follow are unchanged.
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="temperance_article_read">
<Markdown id="temperance_article_md">
## The Magazine Article — On Paper

Your instructor handed out a printed copy of the magazine article about the
"sober-curious" movement. **Read it on paper now.**

Read it the same way you read the textbook on screen — with a pen or a
highlighter in hand:

- **Mark the three words** the article's introduction asks you to watch for:
  *teetotaling*, *self-optimization*, and *authenticity.*
- **Underline** anything that says what today's movement wants people to do
  about alcohol, and why.
- **Star** anything that reminds you of the *American Yawp* excerpt or the
  lithograph — you'll need those connections shortly.

Keep the handout beside you. The questions in the conversation on the left are
all about it, and you'll draw on it again when you pull the three texts
together at the end.

*If you don't have a copy, ask your instructor before going on.*
</Markdown>
<!-- The reading happens off-screen, so the chat has nothing to watch except
       this: the student tells us when the paper article has been read. -->
<Done id="temperance_article_read_done" label="I've read the handout" />
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_article_read -->


<!-- ============================================================
     Part 4 — The lithograph (third text). PDF p.42 planned it; the
     image is the one on the last page of "Clean Copy SBA Temperance
     Materials 3.24.docx": N. Currier's 1846 "The Drunkard's Progress."
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="temperance_image">
<Markdown id="temperance_image_intro">
## A Temperance-Era Image

Study this 19th-century lithograph. Notice the **arch** shape, the **nine
numbered steps**, and the scene **beneath the arch.**
</Markdown>
<Image id="temperance_image_figure" src="images/drunkards_progress.jpg" width="820"
  alt="'The Drunkard's Progress' (N. Currier, 1846): nine captioned figures form an arch rising on the left and falling on the right, tracing a man from a first friendly drink to death; beneath the arch a woman and child leave a ruined home."
  authors="N. Currier, 1846" license="Public domain" />
<Markdown id="temperance_image_caption">
**"The Drunkard's Progress, from the first glass to the grave"** — N. Currier, 1846.

The nine steps, in order:

1. A glass with a friend.
2. A glass to keep the cold out.
3. A glass too much.
4. Drunk and riotous.
5. The summit attained. Jolly companions. A confirmed drunkard.
6. Poverty and disease.
7. Forsaken by friends.
8. Desperation and crime.
9. Death by suicide.

*Beneath the arch, a woman and child leave a ruined home.*
</Markdown>
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_image -->


<!-- ============================================================
     Part 4 — Synthesis: sort each description as "then" (temperance)
     vs "now" (sober-curious), drawing on all three texts. Graded with
     the assessment attributes (see the vocabulary items).
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="temperance_synth_sort">
<Markdown id="temperance_synth_sort_prompt">
## Then and Now

Using the textbook, the printed article, and the lithograph, sort each description under
the movement it best fits. Then press **Submit.**
</Markdown>
<CapaProblem id="temperance_synth_sort_problem" title="Then vs. Now" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<TabularMCQGrader id="temperance_synth_sort_grader">
<TabularMCQ id="temperance_synth_sort_grid">
cols:
  - "19th-century temperance movement"
  - "Today's sober-curious movement"
rows:
  - "Focused on the individual's own health and self-optimization|s_health[Today's sober-curious movement]"
  - "Grew out of religious revival and the campaign for women's rights|s_revival[19th-century temperance movement]"
  - "Framed as authenticity, self-care, and a personal 'freedom to choose'|s_auth[Today's sober-curious movement]"
  - "Used images like 'The Drunkard's Progress' to warn of moral ruin|s_litho[19th-century temperance movement]"
  - "Framed as protecting the home, the family, and the nation|s_protect[19th-century temperance movement]"
</TabularMCQ>
</TabularMCQGrader>
</CapaProblem>
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_synth_sort -->


<!-- ============================================================
     Part 5 — Culminating writing task (Pulling It Together).
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="temperance_final">
<Markdown id="temperance_final_prompt">
## Pulling It Together

This is the goal the whole module has been building toward.

**In four or five sentences, explain the goals of the 1800s temperance movement
and of the modern "sober-curious" movement. Then compare and contrast them: in
what ways are the two movements alike, and how do they differ?**
</Markdown>
<TextArea id="temperance_final_write" rows="8"
  placeholder="Both movements discouraged drinking, but…" />

<!-- Progress affordance, two tiers. There is no longer a word minimum to
       hit: the conversation moves on as soon as the student has really begun,
       so tier 1 is encouragement rather than a countdown. The live word count
       is gone with it — a number under a textarea reads as a target to reach
       and stop at, which is exactly the behaviour a hard minimum produced.
       The second tier only ever claims what it actually checked (length);
       quality is handed back to the student as a read-it-back checklist. A
       former third tier said "✓ That's a full comparison" at 80 words, which
       was dishonest — length isn't a comparison. -->
<Markdown when="wordcount(@temperance_final_write.value) &lt; 40">
*A solid comparison usually takes four or five sentences — keep going.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="wordcount(@temperance_final_write.value) &gt;= 40">
*✓ You can continue. Before you do, read it back: does it name **one way the movements are alike** and **one way they differ**? Does it draw on **all three texts** — the textbook, the lithograph, and the article?*
</Markdown>
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_final -->


<!-- ============================================================
     Part 5 — Conclusion / wrap-up (replaces the old in-progress note).
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="temperance_conclusion">
<Markdown id="temperance_conclusion_md">
## You're Done

Nice work — you've finished the module. Along the way you practiced the full
range of READ 1000 reading skills on **three real texts:**

- **using context for vocabulary** (antidote, galvanized, per capita, and more)
- **close, academic-language reading** (the subject–verb–object of a hard sentence, and its antecedent)
- **reading comprehension** across the textbook and the article
- **organizing notes** under headings
- **analyzing an image** as persuasion, and finally
- **synthesizing** all three texts into four or five sentences comparing the historic temperance movement with today's approaches to drinking less.

Here is the finished work — the comparison you built from all three texts.
</Markdown>
<!-- The writing screen is behind us in the pane by now, so the conclusion
       shows the comparison rather than pointing at a screen that has moved
       on. <Use>, not a copy: same block, same state, still editable. -->
<Use ref="temperance_final_write" />
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_conclusion -->


