     ============================================================ -->

<!-- Each chat-embedded question is wrapped in a Vertical that TAKES OVER the
     embed id (`::temperance_vocab_commerce_screen`), so the correctness feedback under
     the choices renders inside the chat bubble with them. The ChoiceInput
     keeps its own id — every wait, and every readback, still names it.

     Inside that Vertical the question is a CapaProblem (`<id>_problem`)
     around a KeyGrader around the ChoiceInput, carrying the four assessment
     attributes every scored item in this form carries:

         maxAttempts="1"          one submission; "0" would mean unlimited
         showAnswer="attempted"   the key becomes available on that submission
         answerReveal="auto"      and appears by itself — there is no Show
                                  Answer button to find
         lockInput="closed"       the input freezes once attempts are used,
                                  so what is on screen is what was scored

     The Submit button is the CapaProblem's own footer. The ChoiceInput id and
     every option value are unchanged — the chatpeg gates and the analytics
     read them.

     Every per-choice verdict below is gated on `<id>_problem.submitCount` as
     well as on the chosen value. Keyed on the value alone, a student with a
     Submit button in front of them could click each option in turn and read
     which one comes back "Yes" before ever submitting — the whole item,
     answered for them, under a rule that allows one attempt.

     Register: each line is the explanation that follows a submission, not a
     hint toward a second try — there is none. A wrong-choice line says why
     that option misses and what the key is; nothing tells the student to
     look again. -->

<Vertical id="temperance_vocab_commerce_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_vocab_commerce_problem" title="Vocabulary: commerce" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_vocab_commerce_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_vocab_commerce">
<Markdown>
Look at the highlighted sentence and choose the best definition for **commerce**:

> "…the Industrial Revolution had led to a host of social problems associated
> with urban growth and large-scale **commerce**."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_commerce_church_formation" value="church_formation"> The way Christianity formed in the U.S.        </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_commerce_population_study" value="population_study"> The study of how urban populations grow.       </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_vocab_commerce_buying_selling"   value="buying_selling"  > The buying and selling of products.            </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_commerce_social_laws"      value="social_laws"     > The laws people make to limit social problems. </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_commerce.value === 'church_formation' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_commerce_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's the religious half of the paragraph. "Large-scale commerce" sits next to "urban growth" — both are economic changes the Industrial Revolution brought, and commerce is the trade: buying and selling.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_commerce.value === 'population_study' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_commerce_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's the "urban growth" half of the sentence. "Commerce" is the other half — buying and selling, not counting people.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_commerce.value === 'social_laws' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_commerce_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Laws come later in the chapter, with prohibition. "Commerce" names the economic activity itself — buying and selling — not the rules made about it.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_commerce.value === 'buying_selling' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_commerce_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — commerce is buying and selling. "Large-scale commerce" means trade grown big enough to reshape whole cities.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_vocab_commerce_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_vocab_commerce_screen -->

<Vertical id="temperance_vocab_antidote_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_vocab_antidote_problem" title="Vocabulary: antidote" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_vocab_antidote_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_vocab_antidote">
<Markdown>
Choose the best definition for **antidote**:

> "The Second Great Awakening was in part a spiritual response to such changes…
> providing an institutional **antidote** to the insecurities of a rapidly
> changing world."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_antidote_anecdote_story"      value="anecdote_story"     > A short story about a real person or event. </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_antidote_poison_medicine"     value="poison_medicine"    > A medicine that saves you from poison.      </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_antidote_unpublished_writing" value="unpublished_writing"> An unpublished piece of writing.            </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_vocab_antidote_counteracts_harm"    value="counteracts_harm"   > Something that works against a harm.        </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<!-- The peers unpack the two "anecdote" definitions and the medicine reading in
   the dialogue right after this, so the feedback here stays short. -->
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_antidote.value === 'anecdote_story' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_antidote_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's an **anecdote** — one letter off, an entirely different word. An antidote works against a harm; here, against the insecurities of a changing world.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_antidote.value === 'unpublished_writing' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_antidote_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*A definition of some other word — nothing in "antidote" is about writing. An antidote works against a harm; here, against the insecurities of a changing world.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_antidote.value === 'poison_medicine' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_antidote_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*A real meaning of the word, but the literal one: the Awakening was no medicine. Here the sense is figurative — something that works against a harm, the insecurities of a changing world.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_antidote.value === 'counteracts_harm' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_antidote_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — the figurative sense: something that works against a harm. Here, against the insecurities of a changing world.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_vocab_antidote_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_vocab_antidote_screen -->

<Vertical id="temperance_vocab_proliferated_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_vocab_proliferated_problem" title="Vocabulary: proliferated" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_vocab_proliferated_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_vocab_proliferated">
<Markdown>
Choose the best definition for **proliferated**:

> "These new societies **proliferated** in the United States between 1815 and
> 1861…"
</Markdown>
         <Key id="temperance_vocab_proliferated_increased_rapidly"   value="increased_rapidly"  > Increased rapidly in numbers.                </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_proliferated_merged_into_one"     value="merged_into_one"    > Came together to form a single organization. </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_proliferated_decreased_influence" value="decreased_influence"> Decreased rapidly in influence.              </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_proliferated_moved_to_cities"     value="moved_to_cities"    > Moved from farms to major cities.            </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_proliferated.value === 'merged_into_one' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_proliferated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Nearly the opposite: to proliferate is to multiply. These societies spread rather than folding into one body.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_proliferated.value === 'decreased_influence' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_proliferated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*"Rapidly" is right; the direction isn't. To proliferate is to increase fast — between 1815 and 1861 the societies were multiplying, not fading.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_proliferated.value === 'moved_to_cities' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_proliferated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's the urban growth from the paragraph before. "Proliferated" is about number, not location: the societies increased rapidly.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_proliferated.value === 'increased_rapidly' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_proliferated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — to proliferate is to multiply fast. The societies didn't just appear; they kept appearing.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_vocab_proliferated_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_vocab_proliferated_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — vocabulary, chunk B / para 3 (PDF p.18) -->

<Vertical id="temperance_vocab_galvanized_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_vocab_galvanized_problem" title="Vocabulary: galvanized" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_vocab_galvanized_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_vocab_galvanized">
<Markdown>
What is the best definition of **galvanized**?

> "…the movement's effort to curb the consumption of alcohol **galvanized**
> widespread support among the middle class."
</Markdown>
         <Key id="temperance_vocab_galvanized_spurred_into_action" value="spurred_into_action"> To spur into action; to energize.       </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_galvanized_zinc_coating"        value="zinc_coating"       > To coat iron or steel with zinc.        </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_galvanized_gradually_built"     value="gradually_built"    > To build up slowly, a little at a time. </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_galvanized_wore_down"           value="wore_down"          > To discourage or wear down.             </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<!-- Karis and Danielle name the figurative definition in the dialogue that
   follows, so these lines stay short. The old options
   were all worded around "electric shock," which sent students hunting for a
   literal current instead of reading the sentence; the shock framing is gone. -->
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_galvanized.value === 'gradually_built' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_galvanized_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The pace is wrong. To galvanize is to spur into action — the support was energized all at once, not built up slowly.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_galvanized.value === 'zinc_coating' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_galvanized_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's galvanized steel, a real but different sense of the word. Here the sense is figurative: the effort spurred the middle class into action.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_galvanized.value === 'wore_down' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_galvanized_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Backwards. The sentence says the movement's effort won widespread support — it spurred people into action rather than wearing them down.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_galvanized.value === 'spurred_into_action' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_galvanized_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — the effort spurred the middle class into action and energized their support.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_vocab_galvanized_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_vocab_galvanized_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — vocabulary, chunk C / para 4 (PDF p.19-21) -->

<Vertical id="temperance_vocab_eradicating_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_vocab_eradicating_problem" title="Vocabulary: eradicating" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_vocab_eradicating_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_vocab_eradicating">
<Markdown>
What words could take the place of **eradicating** — but keep the meaning the same?

> "…women formed a significant presence in societies dedicated to
> **eradicating** liquor."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_eradicating_making_rules"        value="making_rules"       > making rules for     </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_eradicating_advertising"         value="advertising"        > helping to advertise </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_vocab_eradicating_completely_removing" value="completely_removing"> completely removing  </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_eradicating_keeping_safe"        value="keeping_safe"       > keeping safe from    </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_eradicating.value === 'making_rules' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_eradicating_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Regulating liquor is a milder goal than the one the sentence describes. To eradicate is to remove completely — these societies wanted liquor gone, not managed.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_eradicating.value === 'advertising' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_eradicating_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The opposite of what a temperance society was dedicated to: eradicating liquor means removing it completely.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_eradicating.value === 'keeping_safe' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_eradicating_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*"Dedicated to keeping liquor safe" turns the sentence around. The societies were against liquor — eradicating it means removing it completely.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_eradicating.value === 'completely_removing' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_eradicating_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — to eradicate is to root out entirely. That's why it goes with "full abstinence" rather than "moderation."*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_vocab_eradicating_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_vocab_eradicating_screen -->

<Vertical id="temperance_vocab_exacerbated_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_vocab_exacerbated_problem" title="Vocabulary: exacerbated" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_vocab_exacerbated_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_vocab_exacerbated">
<Markdown>
What words could take the place of **exacerbated** — but keep the meaning the same?

> "Such intrusions by the Protestant middle class **exacerbated** class, ethnic,
> and religious tensions."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_exacerbated_calmed_down"  value="calmed_down" > calmed right down </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_vocab_exacerbated_made_worse"   value="made_worse"  > made much worse   </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_exacerbated_fixed"        value="fixed"       > fixed right up    </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_exacerbated_started_over" value="started_over"> started over      </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_exacerbated.value === 'calmed_down' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_exacerbated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Backwards. If the intrusions had calmed things, the paragraph wouldn't be about tensions at all — to exacerbate is to make something worse.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_exacerbated.value === 'fixed' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_exacerbated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The wrong direction — nothing here gets repaired. The paragraph describes friction getting worse, not its cure.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_exacerbated.value === 'started_over' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_exacerbated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The class, ethnic, and religious tensions were already there. The intrusions made them worse — a change of degree, not a new start.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_exacerbated.value === 'made_worse' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_exacerbated_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — to exacerbate is to make something bad that already exists worse.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_vocab_exacerbated_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_vocab_exacerbated_screen -->

<Vertical id="temperance_vocab_per_capita_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_vocab_per_capita_problem" title="Vocabulary: per capita" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_vocab_per_capita_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_vocab_per_capita">
<Markdown>
What words could take the place of **per capita consumption** — but keep the meaning the same?

> "In the 1840s, Americans drank half of what they had in the 1820s, and
> **per capita** consumption continued to decline over the next two decades."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_per_capita_per_saloon"       value="per_saloon"      > the amount of drinking within each saloon       </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_per_capita_nationwide_total" value="nationwide_total"> the amount of drinking across the whole country </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_per_capita_per_ethnic_group" value="per_ethnic_group"> the amount of drinking by each ethnic group     </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_vocab_per_capita_per_person"       value="per_person"      > the average amount of drinking per person       </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_per_capita.value === 'per_saloon' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_per_capita_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*"Capita" is Latin for heads — people, not places. Per capita consumption is the average amount per person.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_per_capita.value === 'nationwide_total' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_per_capita_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*A national total would climb just because the population climbed. Per capita divides by the people — the average per person — which is what makes the drop meaningful.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_per_capita.value === 'per_ethnic_group' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_per_capita_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Groups do show up elsewhere in this paragraph, but "per capita" counts by head — the average per person.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_per_capita.value === 'per_person' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_per_capita_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — the average per person. Take all the alcohol drunk in the country and divide it by everyone in it: the average American in the 1840s drank half of what the average American drank in the 1820s.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_vocab_per_capita_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_vocab_per_capita_screen -->


<!-- ============================================================
     CHAT-EMBEDDED — Benevolent Empire, disciplinary term (PDF p.22-24)
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="temperance_term_pick_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_term_pick_problem" title="Benevolent Empire: the term" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_term_pick_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_term_pick">
<Markdown>
**Benevolent Empire** is a discipline-specific term. Using clues in the passage,
which word or phrase most directly helps us understand what it was?
</Markdown>
         <Key id="temperance_term_pick_powerful_network"      value="powerful_network"     > powerful network      </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_term_pick_industrial_revolution" value="industrial_revolution"> Industrial Revolution </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_term_pick_traditional_religion"  value="traditional_religion" > traditional religion  </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_term_pick_spiritual_response"    value="spiritual_response"   > spiritual response    </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_term_pick.value === 'traditional_religion' &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The passage says traditional Protestantism was "at low tide." The Benevolent Empire is the new thing, not the old one — the "powerful network" of societies that grew in its place.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_term_pick.value === 'industrial_revolution' &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's the cause of the social problems the reformers answered, not a name for the reformers' network. The phrase the passage gives for that is "a powerful network."*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_term_pick.value === 'spiritual_response' &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That phrase describes the Second Great Awakening. The Benevolent Empire is the organized thing the Awakening produced — "a powerful network" of societies.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_term_pick.value === 'powerful_network' &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — the passage hands you the phrase: "a powerful network." Many societies, linked together, melding religion and reform.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_term_pick_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_term_pick_screen -->

<Vertical id="temperance_term_connect_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_term_connect_problem" title="Benevolent Empire: what describes it" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<CheckboxGrader id="temperance_term_connect_grader">
<CheckboxInput id="temperance_term_connect">
<Markdown>
The Benevolent Empire was, indeed, a **powerful network.** Which of these words or
phrases from the chapter describe the network itself? **Select all that apply.**
</Markdown>
         <Key id="temperance_term_connect_societies_movements"     value="societies_movements"    > societies / movements   </Key>
         <Key id="temperance_term_connect_middle_class"            value="middle_class"           > middle-class            </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_term_connect_american_independence"   value="american_independence"  > American independence   </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_term_connect_commercial_distilleries" value="commercial_distilleries"> commercial distilleries </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_term_connect_religion_and_reform"     value="religion_and_reform"    > religion and reform     </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_term_connect_irish_catholics"         value="irish_catholics"        > Irish Catholics         </Distractor>
</CheckboxInput>
</CheckboxGrader>

<!-- Multi-select, so the feedback has to cover three cases: a wrong pick, a
   right-but-incomplete set, and the whole set.

   SCORING RULE: exact match. CheckboxGrader with no partialCredit marks the
   response correct only when all three Keys and no Distractor are selected;
   anything else is simply wrong. That is a strict rule for a six-option
   select-all, and it is declared here as the rule this form scores by, not
   as the only defensible one — partial credit is the obvious next question. -->
<Markdown when="@temperance_term_connect.value.includes('american_independence') &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*American independence is the era the chapter opens in — the backdrop, not a piece of the network.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_term_connect.value.includes('commercial_distilleries') &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Distilleries are what the reformers were up against — the target of the network, not part of it.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_term_connect.value.includes('irish_catholics') &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Irish Catholic workers are the people the middle-class reformers intruded on: the other side of the tension, not part of the network.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_term_connect.value.length &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_connect.value.length &lt; 3 &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Fewer than the full set. Three phrases describe the network: who was in it (middle-class), how it was organized (societies / movements), and what it joined together (religion and reform).*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_term_connect.value.length === 3 &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_connect.value.includes('societies_movements') &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_connect.value.includes('middle_class') &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_connect.value.includes('religion_and_reform') &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's the set: middle-class people, organized into societies and movements, melding religion and reform.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_term_connect_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_term_connect_screen -->

<!-- Three items in this form ask which peer's sentence is best (this one, the
     self-optimization sentence, the closing comparison). Each peer is right in
     exactly one of them: a peer who is reliably right is a cue that has nothing
     to do with reading. Option values name the sentence, not the speaker, so
     an attribution can move without touching values, gates, or analytics. -->
<Vertical id="temperance_term_bestdef_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_term_bestdef_problem" title="Benevolent Empire: the best definition" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_term_bestdef_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_term_bestdef">
<Markdown>
Each member of your group wrote a one-sentence description of the Benevolent
Empire. Read them and choose the **best** one.
</Markdown>
         <Key id="temperance_term_bestdef_network_religion_reform"  value="network_religion_reform" > **Danielle:** A network of religious societies that joined religion and reform among middle-class Evangelicals.       </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_term_bestdef_evangelicals_name"        value="evangelicals_name"       > **Joe:** The name given to the Evangelicals in the temperance movement after the Second Great Awakening.              </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_term_bestdef_reforms_promote_religion" value="reforms_promote_religion"> **Karis:** The set of reforms designed to promote religious ideas that had decreased after the Industrial Revolution. </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_term_bestdef.value === 'evangelicals_name' &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_bestdef_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Joe names who was in it, but not what it **was** — and the Benevolent Empire was much wider than temperance alone. Danielle's sentence has the missing piece: a network of societies joining religion and reform.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_term_bestdef.value === 'reforms_promote_religion' &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_bestdef_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Karis has the engine running backwards. Religious revival came first and powered the reform, rather than reform being invented to prop religion back up — Danielle's sentence has the order right.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_term_bestdef.value === 'network_religion_reform' &amp;&amp; @temperance_term_bestdef_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — Danielle's has all three pieces: a network, made of societies, joining religion and reform among the middle class.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_term_bestdef_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_term_bestdef_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — why did the intrusions exacerbate tensions? (PDF p.29) -->

<Vertical id="temperance_gram_why_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_gram_why_problem" title="Why the intrusions exacerbated tensions" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_gram_why_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_gram_why">
<Markdown>
So why did those intrusions — like the temperance campaigns — *exacerbate*
class, ethnic, and religious tensions?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_gram_why_money_to_saloons"       value="money_to_saloons"      > Because reformers gave money directly to saloon owners instead of working-class people.                        </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_gram_why_only_irish_affected"    value="only_irish_affected"   > Because the campaigns ended up affecting only the Irish Catholics but not the working-class people.            </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_gram_why_irish_led_movement"     value="irish_led_movement"    > Because Irish Catholic immigrants led the temperance movement themselves, against the Protestant middle class. </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_gram_why_middle_class_intrusion" value="middle_class_intrusion"> Because middle-class Protestant reformers intruded on the customs of working-class and immigrant communities.  </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_gram_why.value === 'money_to_saloons' &amp;&amp; @temperance_gram_why_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*No money changes hands in this passage, and money isn't what the tension is about. It's about one group — middle-class Protestant reformers — reaching into the customs of another.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_gram_why.value === 'only_irish_affected' &amp;&amp; @temperance_gram_why_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The sentence names Irish Catholics as many of the lower-class workers, not as the only people affected. The friction runs between classes: middle-class reformers intruding on working-class communities.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_gram_why.value === 'irish_led_movement' &amp;&amp; @temperance_gram_why_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The other way around: temperance was "championed by the middle class," and it threatened to intrude on lower-class workers, many of whom were Irish Catholics.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_gram_why.value === 'middle_class_intrusion' &amp;&amp; @temperance_gram_why_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — one group reaching into another group's private life is exactly what makes class, ethnic, and religious tensions worse.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_gram_why_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_gram_why_screen -->


