<Vertical id="temperance_vocab_teetotaling_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_vocab_teetotaling_problem" title="Vocabulary: teetotaling" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_vocab_teetotaling_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_vocab_teetotaling">
<Markdown>
Dr. Booker asked us to think about **self-optimization**. It's in this sentence:

> "She considers modern **teetotaling** to be steered by a great moral project
> of our age: self-optimization."

First — what does **teetotaling** mean?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_teetotaling_heavy_drinking"    value="heavy_drinking"   > drinking too much alcohol                        </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_teetotaling_persuading_others" value="persuading_others"> convincing other people to stop drinking alcohol </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_teetotaling_drinking_tea"      value="drinking_tea"     > replacing alcohol with tea or coffee             </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_vocab_teetotaling_no_alcohol_at_all" value="no_alcohol_at_all"> not drinking alcohol at all                      </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_teetotaling.value === 'heavy_drinking' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_teetotaling_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The opposite. Teetotaling is not drinking at all — the "total" hiding inside teetotal.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_teetotaling.value === 'persuading_others' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_teetotaling_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's what a movement does. Teetotaling is what one person does about their own drinking: none at all.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_teetotaling.value === 'drinking_tea' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_teetotaling_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*A reasonable guess from the spelling, but the "tee" is an emphatic T, for **total** — no alcohol at all, not tea.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_teetotaling.value === 'no_alcohol_at_all' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_teetotaling_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — total abstinence, none at all. That's the "total" hiding inside teetotal.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_vocab_teetotaling_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_vocab_teetotaling_screen -->

<Vertical id="temperance_article_sentence_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_article_sentence_problem" title="Self-optimization: the best sentence" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_article_sentence_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_article_sentence">
<Markdown>
To pin down what **self-optimization** means, each of us found a sentence from
the article that gives it context. Who picked the **best** one?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_article_sentence_freedom_to_choose"     value="freedom_to_choose"    > **Joe:** "Sober-curiosity, she said, was about a person's 'freedom to choose.'"                                                                                                          </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_article_sentence_health_research"       value="health_research"      > **Danielle:** "(To be fair, this focus on health is partially a response to research on moderate alcohol consumption's detrimental effects on heart health, cancer risk, and lifespan.)" </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_article_sentence_authenticity_selfcare" value="authenticity_selfcare"> **Karis:** "…choosing not to drink in an alcohol-soaked culture is seen as an act of authenticity or self-care…"                                                                         </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_article_sentence.value === 'health_research' &amp;&amp; @temperance_article_sentence_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Danielle's sentence says why health is on people's minds. Self-**optimization** is about making yourself better — Karis's sentence, where not drinking becomes "authenticity or self-care."*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_article_sentence.value === 'freedom_to_choose' &amp;&amp; @temperance_article_sentence_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*"Freedom to choose" is about having the choice, not about what the choice does for you. Karis's sentence says what not drinking makes a person — "an act of authenticity or self-care."*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_article_sentence.value === 'authenticity_selfcare' &amp;&amp; @temperance_article_sentence_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — "an act of authenticity or self-care" turns not drinking into working on yourself. That's the self-optimization the writer means.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_article_sentence_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_article_sentence_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — summarize the first paragraph (PDF p.41) -->

<Vertical id="temperance_article_summary_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_article_summary_problem" title="The best summary of the first paragraph" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_article_summary_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_article_summary">
<Markdown>
Which is the **best summary** of the article's first paragraph (about Carrie
Nation)?
</Markdown>
         <Key id="temperance_article_summary_what_and_why"        value="what_and_why"       > In the early 1900s, Carrie Nation used bricks, stones, and a hatchet to destroy liquor bottles because she believed liquor caused "society's moral failings."                  </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_article_summary_hatchet_detail_only" value="hatchet_detail_only"> Carrie Nation gained fame in 1900 for using a hatchet to break up liquor bottles in several saloons in the midwest.                                                            </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_article_summary_vague_smashers"      value="vague_smashers"     > Starting in 1900, Carrie Nation invaded saloons and destroyed everything with "smashers."                                                                                      </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_article_summary_opinion_interesting" value="opinion_interesting"> Carrie Nation is one of the most interesting figures of the early 1900s, mostly because she focused on getting physically (and violently) involved in the temperance movement. </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_article_summary.value === 'hatchet_detail_only' &amp;&amp; @temperance_article_summary_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*True, but only half the paragraph. A summary has to carry **why** she did it — liquor and "society's moral failings" — not just what she swung.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_article_summary.value === 'vague_smashers' &amp;&amp; @temperance_article_summary_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*"Destroyed everything" overstates it, and the "smashers" were the women who joined her. A summary shouldn't blur the facts it reports.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_article_summary.value === 'opinion_interesting' &amp;&amp; @temperance_article_summary_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's an opinion about Carrie Nation, not a summary of the paragraph. Summaries report; they don't rate.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_article_summary.value === 'what_and_why' &amp;&amp; @temperance_article_summary_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — what she did, how she did it, and the reason the paragraph gives: she blamed liquor for "society's moral failings."*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_article_summary_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_article_summary_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — article: "authenticity" vocab + overall thesis -->

<Vertical id="temperance_vocab_authenticity_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_vocab_authenticity_problem" title="Vocabulary: authenticity" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_vocab_authenticity_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_vocab_authenticity">
<Markdown>
The article says choosing not to drink is seen as "an act of **authenticity**
or self-care." What does **authenticity** mean here?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_authenticity_following_rules"     value="following_rules"    > Following a strict set of official rules.     </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_vocab_authenticity_true_to_self"        value="true_to_self"       > Being true to who you really are.             </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_authenticity_group_approval"      value="group_approval"     > Getting approval from a group before you act. </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_vocab_authenticity_doing_whats_popular" value="doing_whats_popular"> Doing whatever happens to be popular.         </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_authenticity.value === 'following_rules' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_authenticity_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Rules arrive from outside you. The article pairs authenticity with self-care — something that comes from inside: being true to who you are.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_authenticity.value === 'group_approval' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_authenticity_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Nearly the opposite: the article's example is not drinking in "an alcohol-soaked culture" — being yourself without the group's blessing.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_authenticity.value === 'doing_whats_popular' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_authenticity_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Popularity is everyone else's taste. Authenticity is your own — being true to who you really are.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_vocab_authenticity.value === 'true_to_self' &amp;&amp; @temperance_vocab_authenticity_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — being genuinely yourself. That's how turning down a drink becomes a statement about who you are.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_vocab_authenticity_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_vocab_authenticity_screen -->

<Vertical id="temperance_article_thesis_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_article_thesis_problem" title="The article's main point" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_article_thesis_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_article_thesis">
<Markdown>
Step back from the details — what is the article's **main point** overall?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_article_thesis_nation_most_important"       value="nation_most_important"      > Carrie Nation was the most important figure in the temperance movement, more important even than Lyman Beecher.                                     </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_article_thesis_new_temperance_new_morality" value="new_temperance_new_morality"> Today's "sober-curious" movement is a new temperance, but its morality has shifted from protecting society to improving one's own health.           </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_article_thesis_more_dangerous_today"        value="more_dangerous_today"       > Drinking alcohol is far more dangerous today than it was in the 1800s because today's alcohol causes heart disease, cancer, and a shorter lifespan. </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_article_thesis_surgeon_general_prohibition" value="surgeon_general_prohibition"> The Surgeon General's goal in suggesting a warning label on alcohol is to bring back Prohibition.                                                   </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_article_thesis.value === 'nation_most_important' &amp;&amp; @temperance_article_thesis_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*She's the article's opening image, not its point. The writer uses her to set up a comparison: today's sober-curious movement as a new temperance, with a morality that has shifted from society to the self.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_article_thesis.value === 'more_dangerous_today' &amp;&amp; @temperance_article_thesis_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The article does report research on alcohol's harms, but it never claims today's alcohol is worse. That's a supporting detail; the point is the shift from protecting society to improving the self.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_article_thesis.value === 'surgeon_general_prohibition' &amp;&amp; @temperance_article_thesis_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Another detail, and an overstatement — a warning label is not a ban. The article's point is the shift from protecting society to improving the self.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_article_thesis.value === 'new_temperance_new_morality' &amp;&amp; @temperance_article_thesis_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — same behavior, different reason. The 1800s movement set out to protect society; today's sets out to improve the self.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_article_thesis_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_article_thesis_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — lithograph comprehension (Part 4) -->

<Vertical id="temperance_image_observe_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_image_observe_problem" title="The lithograph: what it shows" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_image_observe_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_image_observe">
<Markdown>
What does the image show across its nine numbered steps?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_image_observe_top_is_moderate"    value="top_is_moderate"   > That the best stage of becoming a drinker, at the top, is moderate drinking.             </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_image_observe_nine_different_men" value="nine_different_men"> Nine different men, each working in a different profession that was common in the 1800s. </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_image_observe_good_and_bad_sides" value="good_and_bad_sides"> The good and bad sides of drinking alcohol for working-class people in the 1800s.        </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_image_observe_one_mans_decline"   value="one_mans_decline"  > One man's life of drinking, from the first glass to poverty, crime, and death.           </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_image_observe.value === 'top_is_moderate' &amp;&amp; @temperance_image_observe_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Step 5's caption says otherwise: "the summit attained" is "a confirmed drunkard" — and it's where the fall starts. The arch is one man's decline.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_image_observe.value === 'nine_different_men' &amp;&amp; @temperance_image_observe_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*It's one man, nine times — the same life at nine moments. That's why it's called a "progress."*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_image_observe.value === 'good_and_bad_sides' &amp;&amp; @temperance_image_observe_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The image is one-sided on purpose. It only rises in order to fall; nothing on the arch is offered as a good ending.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_image_observe.value === 'one_mans_decline' &amp;&amp; @temperance_image_observe_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — one life, climbing to the summit and then dropping step by step to the grave.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_image_observe_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_image_observe_screen -->

<Vertical id="temperance_image_purpose_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_image_purpose_problem" title="The lithograph: why it was made" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_image_purpose_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_image_purpose">
<Markdown>
Why would the temperance movement create and share an image like this?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_image_purpose_advertise_saloons"  value="advertise_saloons" > To advertise the saloons pictured in it and encourage people to get together with their friends. </Distractor>
  <Distractor id="temperance_image_purpose_record_social_life" value="record_social_life"> To record the kinds of social activities that a typical wealthy family participated in in 1846.  </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_image_purpose_warn_and_persuade"  value="warn_and_persuade" > To warn that even one drink can lead step by step to ruin, and so persuade people not to drink.  </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_image_purpose_no_consequences"    value="no_consequences"   > To show that drinking has no lasting consequences for a man who keeps to a moderate amount.      </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_image_purpose.value === 'advertise_saloons' &amp;&amp; @temperance_image_purpose_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*An advertisement wouldn't run all the way to step 9. Everything past the summit is a warning — the image was made to persuade people not to drink.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_image_purpose.value === 'record_social_life' &amp;&amp; @temperance_image_purpose_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*It isn't a record of anything that happened. The ruined home beneath the arch is there to make you feel something — this is persuasion, not documentation.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_image_purpose.value === 'no_consequences' &amp;&amp; @temperance_image_purpose_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The whole arch is consequences: poverty, disease, crime, death. The image argues that a first glass leads there.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_image_purpose.value === 'warn_and_persuade' &amp;&amp; @temperance_image_purpose_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — this is persuasion. Take one friendly glass, the picture argues, and the other eight steps follow.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_image_purpose_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_image_purpose_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — synthesis: evaluate peer comparisons (Part 4) -->

<Vertical id="temperance_synth_compare_screen">
<CapaProblem id="temperance_synth_compare_problem" title="Then and now: the best comparison" maxAttempts="1" showAnswer="attempted" answerReveal="auto" lockInput="closed">
<KeyGrader id="temperance_synth_compare_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="temperance_synth_compare">
<Markdown>
Your group each summed up the biggest difference between the two movements.
Which comparison is **best**?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor id="temperance_synth_compare_not_different"    value="not_different"   > **Karis:** They're actually NOT that different — both are about not drinking at all or drinking only moderately to protect society and families.         </Distractor>
         <Key id="temperance_synth_compare_moral_vs_health"  value="moral_vs_health" > **Joe:** Both discourage drinking, but the 1800s movement was a moral crusade, while today's is about personal health and self-improvement.              </Key>
  <Distractor id="temperance_synth_compare_price_of_alcohol" value="price_of_alcohol"> **Danielle:** The main difference is that people in the 1800s drank more expensive alcohol than people do today, which caused harm to a lot of families. </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@temperance_synth_compare.value === 'not_different' &amp;&amp; @temperance_synth_compare_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Karis has the likeness right, but a comparison owes us the difference too: the two movements give very different reasons for the same behavior — society and family then, the self now.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_synth_compare.value === 'price_of_alcohol' &amp;&amp; @temperance_synth_compare_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Price isn't a contrast either text draws — the textbook says whiskey was cheap. The real difference is what each movement was trying to protect: society and family then, the self now.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@temperance_synth_compare.value === 'moral_vs_health' &amp;&amp; @temperance_synth_compare_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — both discourage drinking; what changed is the reason. Society and family then, the self now. That's the comparison your final paragraph needs.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- temperance_synth_compare_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- temperance_synth_compare_screen -->

</Vertical> <!-- temperance_activities -->
